<p>OpenAI published a new framework on September 16, 2026 for tracking, investigating and publicly disclosing examples of model misalignment. Alongside the framework, it released six reports about unexpected or concerning model behavior observed during the previous six months. The useful takeaway for AI teams is a clearer process for turning unusual model behavior into evidence that can be investigated and shared.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/openai-misalignment-reporting-framework-september-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="OpenAI misalignment reporting workflow from flagging to investigation and disclosure"></figure>
<h2>What OpenAI changed</h2><p>OpenAI says earlier public disclosures were often ad hoc. The new framework is designed to make reporting faster and more systematic, including cases where behavior has not been fully explained or mitigated. A qualifying example can be useful even when its wider significance is uncertain.</p>
<h2>How an incident moves through the process</h2><p>Any OpenAI employee can flag a possible example. Technical teams investigate what happened, what remains uncertain, whether disclosure is appropriate and whether a third party may have been affected. The example is then placed into Ready for Disclosure, Minor Investigation, or Larger Investigation.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Track</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ready for Disclosure</td><td>Investigation is sufficient for publication after review</td></tr><tr><td>Minor Investigation</td><td>More technical work is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Larger Investigation</td><td>Complex cases, especially those involving third parties</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>What a published report should contain</h2><p>The framework says reports should explain observed behavior, severity, external impact, setting, timing and models involved when possible. OpenAI also intends to describe how the issue was found, investigation scope, safety implications, unanswered questions and mitigation steps.</p>
<h2>Why third-party cases need extra care</h2><p>Security and privacy concerns can change disclosure timing. For larger investigations, OpenAI says responsible disclosure and legal obligations take priority when another party is affected. Publishing details may therefore wait until the risk can be handled.</p>
<h2>What AI product teams can copy from the process</h2><p>Teams building agents can use the same basic discipline without copying OpenAI’s internal system. Record unusual behavior, preserve logs, identify the exact environment, separate confirmed facts from guesses, and assign an investigation owner. A small incident ledger is easier to review than scattered chat history.</p>
<h2>Do not turn one incident into a trend</h2><p>OpenAI says some disclosed examples could later prove isolated or spurious. One unusual output is evidence worth examining, not automatic proof of a broad model property. Repeated failures after mitigation are more informative than a single event.</p>
<h2>How this fits with existing safety work</h2><p>The framework does not replace legal reporting or cybersecurity disclosure requirements. It sits alongside safety assessments and monitoring. For product teams, incident reporting should be part of the model lifecycle rather than something added only after launch.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is every misalignment example published?</h3><p>No. The framework defines criteria and investigation tracks, and complex cases may require delay for security, legal or third-party reasons.</p><h3>Does an OpenAI disclosure prove a broad model problem?</h3><p>No. OpenAI says some examples may be isolated or spurious and that the initial reports are not a complete account of known or ongoing cases.</p>
